96-### 1.2. THE COMPANY RULE (1773-1858)
97-
98-- **Phase 1 (1757–1773):** Rule of the East India Company in India - Diarchy (Dual System)
99-- **Phase 2 (1773–1858):** Rule of the Company under Parliamentary control
100-
101-#### 1.2.1. Regulating Act of 1773
102-- **Background**: To regulate the affairs of the East India Company and to establish control over its administration in India.
103-- **Provisions**:
104-  1. **Governor-General of Bengal**: Designated as Governor-General of Bengal (Lord Warren Hastings was the first). 
105-  2. **Executive Council**: 4 members appointed to assist the Governor-General.
106-  3. **Supreme Court at Calcutta**: Established in 1774 with a Chief Justice and 3 other judges.
107-  4. **Reporting**: The Court of Directors of the Company were required to report its revenue, civil and military affairs in India to the British Government.
108-- **Significance**: First step by British Parliament to regulate Company’s affairs; marked beginning of central administration.
109-- **Drawbacks**: Vague powers of Governor-General and Council, jurisdictional conflicts between Governor-General-in-Council and Supreme Court.
110-
111-#### 1.2.2. Pitt's India Act of 1784 (Also called East India Company Act, 1784)
112-- **Objective**: To rectify defects of Regulating Act of 1773; distinguish between commercial and political functions.
113-- **Provisions**:
114-  1. **Board of Control**: Created in Britain to control political affairs of Company (6 members including Chancellor of Exchequer and Secretary of State).
115-  2. **Court of Directors**: Left to manage commercial affairs.
116-  3. **Governor-General in Council**: Given more powers, became subordinate to Board of Control.
117-  4. **British territories in India**: Called "British possessions in India".
118-- **Significance**: Introduced the system of Double Government (Dual Control) - Company as administrative agent but under Crown's control through Board of Control.
119-- **Drawbacks**: System of double government was complex and led to confusion.
120-
121-#### 1.2.3. Charter Act of 1793
122-- **Provisions**: Extended Company's charter for 20 years; gave more powers to Governor-General over Governors of Madras and Bombay; Royal approval mandatory for Governor-General's appointment; salaries of Board of Control members to be paid from Indian revenues.
123-- **Significance**: Strengthened centralisation.
124-
125-#### 1.2.4. Charter Act of 1813
126-- **Provisions**:
127-  1. **End of Monopoly**: Company's monopoly over Indian trade ended except trade in tea and trade with China.
128-  2. **Sovereignty**: Asserted British Crown's sovereignty over Company's territories.
129-  3. **Missionaries**: Allowed Christian missionaries to come to India for propagation of Christianity.
130-  4. **Education**: Rs. 1 lakh per year sanctioned for promotion of literature and education of Indians.
131-- **Significance**: India opened to British merchants; beginning of missionary activities and modern education.
132-
133-#### 1.2.5. Charter Act of 1833 (Great Charter Act)
134-- **Provisions**:
135-  1. **End of Commercial Functions**: Company ceased to be a commercial body, became purely administrative.
136-  2. **Governor-General of India**: Governor-General of Bengal became Governor-General of India (Lord William Bentinck first).
137-  3. **Centralisation**: All civil and military powers vested in Governor-General-in-Council.
138-  4. **Law Member**: 4th member added to Council as Law Member (Lord Macaulay first).
139-  5. **Indian Civil Services**: Open competition recommended (later negated), but provision made.
140-  6. **Equality Clause**: Stated Indians not to be debarred from holding offices on grounds of religion, colour, birth etc.
141-- **Significance**: Most important act before 1853 - final step towards centralisation; attempted to liberalise administrative structure.
142-
143-#### 1.2.6. Charter Act of 1853
144-- **Provisions**:
145-  1. **Legislative Separation**: Legislative and executive functions of Governor-General's Council separated; Legislative Council (Indian Legislative Council) created.
146-  2. **Open Competition**: Open competition for Civil Services introduced as per Macaulay's Report.
