## 14. CENTRE-STATE RELATIONS

**[SYL: GS2-B.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 14]**

### 14.1. Introduction
- **Federal Structure**: Constitution of India is federal in structure, divides powers (legislative, executive and financial) between Centre and States
- **Integrated Judiciary**: No division of judicial power - single integrated judicial system enforces both Central and State laws
- **Need for Coordination**: Though supreme in their respective fields, maximum harmony and coordination essential for effective operation of federal system
- **Three Dimensions**: Legislative relations, Administrative relations and Financial relations (Art. 245-293)

### 14.2. LEGISLATIVE RELATIONS (ARTICLES 245-255)

#### 14.2.1. Territorial Extent of Central and State Legislation
- **Parliament**: Can make laws for whole or any part of territory of India (States + UTs + other areas)
- **State Legislature**: Can make laws for whole or any part of the State. Laws not applicable outside state except sufficient nexus between state and object
- **Extrateritorial Legislation**: Only Parliament can make extraterritorial legislation (laws applicable to Indian citizens and their property anywhere in the world)

**Restrictions on Parliament's territorial jurisdiction:**
- **UTs with regulations**: President can make regulations for peace, progress and good government of Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Lakshadweep, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu and Ladakh (have force of Act of Parliament; can repeal/amend Parliamentary acts)
- **Scheduled Areas**: Governor can direct Act of Parliament not apply/apply with modifications to Scheduled Areas in the state
- **Tribal Areas**: Governor of Assam (for tribal areas in Assam) and President (for tribal areas in Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram) can give similar directions

#### 14.2.2. Distribution of Legislative Subjects (Seventh Schedule)
Three-fold distribution:

| List | Authority | Subjects | Count (present) | Examples |
|------|-----------|----------|-----------------|----------|
| **List-I (Union List)** | Parliament - Exclusive | Union subjects | 98 (originally 97) | Defence, Banking, Foreign Affairs, Currency, Atomic Energy, Insurance, Communication, Inter-State Trade & Commerce, Census, Audit |
| **List-II (State List)** | State Legislature - Exclusive (normal circumstances) | State subjects | 59 (originally 66) | Public Order, Police, Public Health & Sanitation, Agriculture, Prisons, Local Govt, Fisheries, Markets, Theatres, Gambling |
| **List-III (Concurrent List)** | Both Parliament and State Legislature | Concurrent subjects | 52 (originally 47) | Criminal law & procedure, Civil procedure, Marriage & Divorce, Population control & Family Planning, Electricity, Labour welfare, Economic & social planning, Drugs, Newspapers/Books/Press, Education, Forests, Weights & Measures, Protection of wild animals/birds, Administration of justice (added by 42nd Amendment 1976) |

- **Residuary Powers**: Art. 248 read with Entry 97 of Union List vests residuary powers with Parliament (unlike USA where residuary with states). 42nd Amendment also gave Parliament power on residuary subjects.

#### 14.2.3. Parliamentary Legislation in the State Field (Art. 249-252)
Under extraordinary circumstances, Parliament can legislate on State List subjects:

| Provision | When Applicable | Effect |
|-----------|-----------------|---------|
| **Art. 249** | Rajya Sabha passes resolution by 2/3rd members present and voting that it is necessary in national interest | Parliament can make laws on State List subjects for whole/any part of India. Resolution remains in force max 1 year (renewable). Becomes inoperative if SR passes contrary resolution. |
| **Art. 250** | During National Emergency (Art. 352) | Parliament gets power to legislate on State List subjects. Continues till 6 months after emergency ends. |
| **Art. 251** | Inconsistency between laws made under Art. 249/250 and State laws | Law made by Parliament prevails; State law void to extent of repugnancy. |
| **Art. 252** | Two or more states pass resolutions requesting Parliament to legislate | Parliament can legislate for those states. Any other state can adopt by passing resolution. Becomes applicable to adopting states. Can be amended/repealed only by Parliament. |
| **Art. 253** | To give effect to international treaties/agreements | Parliament can legislate on any subject even in State List to implement treaties, agreements or conventions with foreign countries. |

#### 14.2.4. Centre's Control Over State Legislation
- **Assent to Bills**: Governor reserves certain bills for President's consideration (Art. 200). President can assent/withhold assent/return (except Money Bills).
- **Bills on certain subjects**: Bills affecting taxation in which states are interested, bills imposing restrictions on freedom of trade/commerce etc may need prior sanction of President (Art. 304(b) proviso etc).
- **Art. 288**: Exemption from taxation by states in certain cases with President's approval
- **Art. 290-A**: Grants from Centre to certain states (Assam, Bengal, Bihar, Orissa) - statutory grants
- **Art. 304**: Restrictions on trade/commercial relations - states can impose restrictions with President's previous sanction
- **Art. 356/360**: During President's Rule/Financial Emergency, Centre's powers expanded

**Doctrine of Repugnancy (Art. 254)**:
- If valid Central law and valid State law on Concurrent List conflict → Central law prevails, State law void to extent of repugnancy
- State law repugnant to earlier Central law can prevail if it has received President's assent (exception)
- In case of repugnancy between State law and law made under Art. 249/250/252/253 → Parliamentary law prevails

